[THOUGHT] The proposed diagnosis of Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN) is strongly supported by the evidence:  
- Classic triad elements present: systemic symptoms (fever, fatigue, flank pain), AKI (↑Cr, ↑urea), and urinary findings (leukocyturia, negative culture);  
- Highly specific urinalysis finding — 3% binucleated cells with eosinophilic, granular cytoplasm — is a well-documented cytologic hallmark of AIN (sensitivity ~70%, specificity >95% in context of AKI);  
- Temporal drug exposure (amoxicillin → cephalexin → omeprazole), rash, and absence of infection/obstruction/glomerular disease further narrow the differential;  
- Key mimics appropriately excluded: normal renal ultrasound (rules out obstruction, structural disease), negative ANCA (lowers likelihood of vasculitis), no hematuria/casts suggesting glomerulonephritis.  
No red-flag findings contradict AIN (e.g., no nephrotic-range proteinuria, no crescents on biopsy [not done but not indicated], no hypocomplementemia or extrarenal vasculitic signs). While renal biopsy remains the gold standard, it is not required when clinical, lab, and urine cytology findings are concordant and alternative diagnoses are robustly excluded — especially given the high pretest probability and drug-hypersensitivity context. All key discriminating features align. Data sufficiency is met at Turn 7; no critical missing information impairs diagnostic confidence.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

